Deze README is bedoeld als praktische handleiding voor ontwikkelaars en testers die nog weinig ervaring hebben met iFrames.
Omdat een iFrame een eigen documentcontext heeft, is het in Chrome DevTools niet mogelijk om met één reguliere CSS- of 
XPath-selector vanuit het hoofddocument rechtstreeks een element binnen een iFrame te selecteren.

Deze handleiding laat zien hoe je de juiste iFrame-context selecteert en vervolgens met eenvoudige 
JavaScript-commando's elementen kunt vinden, uitlezen en controleren. Zo kunnen ook minder ervaren gebruikers 
de locators in frame_counting.resource eenvoudig begrijpen en valideren.

# Frame Counting – Locator Reference

This component automates the Flight Status and Hotel Availability
widgets embedded in separate iframes.

## Inspecting iframe elements

1. Open Chrome DevTools → Console.
2. Select the appropriate iframe context:
   - `flight-status`
   - `hotel-availability`
3. Execute the JavaScript snippets below.

> All selectors are evaluated within the selected iframe.
> The CSS selectors correspond to those used in `frame_counting.resource`.

## Full table
console.table(
  [...document.querySelectorAll('p:has(strong)')].map(row => {
    const number = row.querySelector('strong').textContent.trim();
    const route = row.querySelector('span:first-child').textContent
      .replace(number, '')
      .trim();
    const status = row.querySelector('span:last-child').textContent.trim();

    return {
      flight: number,
      route: route,
      status: status
    };
  })
);

## Flight Status

### Widget title

```javascript
document.querySelector('p.wh')?.textContent.trim()
```

### Flight count

```javascript
document.querySelectorAll('p:has(strong)').length
```

### Flight numbers

```javascript
[...document.querySelectorAll('strong')].map(el => el.textContent.trim())
```

### Flight routes

```javascript
[...document.querySelectorAll('p:has(strong) > span:first-child')]
  .map(el => el.textContent.trim())
```

Note: Route text also contains the flight number.

### Flight statuses

```javascript
[...document.querySelectorAll('p:has(strong) > span:last-child')]
  .map(el => el.textContent.trim())
```

## Hotel Availability

Switch the Console context to `hotel-availability`.

### Widget title

```javascript
document.querySelector('p.wh')?.textContent.trim()
```

### Hotel count

```javascript
document.querySelectorAll('p:has(strong)').length
```

### Hotel names

```javascript
[...document.querySelectorAll('strong')].map(el => el.textContent.trim())
```

### Room availability

```javascript
[...document.querySelectorAll('p:has(strong) > span:last-child')]
  .map(el => el.textContent.trim())
```

## Robot Framework Browser Library

Browser Library uses `>>>` to access elements inside an iframe.

Example:

```robotframework
${FLIGHT_FRAME}    iframe[data-testid="frame-flight-status"]
${FLIGHT_ROWS}     p:has(strong)
```

```robotframework
${count}=    Get Element Count    ${FLIGHT_FRAME} >>> ${FLIGHT_ROWS}
```

The iframe selector identifies the frame, while the element selector
identifies the content inside it.